  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
    "lesson_core_move": "Identify sine and cosine waves at integer multiples of the fundamental frequency as basis functions whose weighted combination represents a periodic signal.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This step is about the basis-function view of the trigonometric Fourier series, not about computing coefficients. The objects to recognize are the sine and cosine waves at integer multiples of the fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$; the coefficients that appear later are weights, not basis functions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(k\\omega_0 t),\\ \\sin(k\\omega_0 t),\\quad k=1,2,3,\\dots",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the first harmonic pair, $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and $\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$. Once you see the $k=1$ case, the same pattern extends to every harmonic by substituting $k=2,3,\\dots$ into the same sine and cosine arguments.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega_0 t),\\ \\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The key action is to separate the basis waves from the coefficients. The basis functions tell you which trigonometric waves are available; the coefficients only tell you how much of each wave is present.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\left[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So when you read a trigonometric Fourier series, identify each harmonic pair by its integer multiple of $\\omega_0$, then treat the coefficients as the weights that build the periodic signal from those basis functions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Fundamental Sine and Cosine Basis Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a periodic signal with fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$, identify the sine and cosine basis functions that make up the first harmonic pair.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is the first harmonic pair, so read the harmonic index as $k=1$. In the trigonometric Fourier-series basis view, each harmonic uses the same two families: the cosine and sine terms at $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(k\\omega_0 t),\\ \\sin(k\\omega_0 t),\\quad k=1,2,3,\\dots",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $k=1$ into both families. That gives the basis functions for the fundamental harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega_0 t),\\ \\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fundamental sine and cosine basis pair is $\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and $\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$. These are the basis waves themselves; the coefficients that appear later are only the weights on these two functions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying Sine and Cosine Basis Functions at Higher Harmonics",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Identify the sine and cosine basis functions for the $k=3$ harmonic of a signal with fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the harmonic index $k=3$. Keep the same basis-function pattern from the earlier example and substitute $k=3$ into the cosine and sine families.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(k\\omega_0 t),\\ \\sin(k\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now replace $k$ with $3$ in both arguments. The harmonic number changes the frequency multiplier, but it does not change the basis-function pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(3\\omega_0 t),\\ \\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the third-harmonic basis pair is $\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)$ and $\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)$. These are the basis waves before any coefficients are applied.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Weights on Harmonic Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the terms $4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)-7\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$, identify the weights on the $k=2$ sine and cosine basis functions.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the harmonic index $k=2$. Read each weight from the number in front of the matching basis function and keep the sign attached to that coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)-7\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cosine term carries the weight $a_2=4$. The sine term carries the weight $b_2=-7$, so the sign stays with the sine coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_2=4,\\quad b_2=-7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the coefficient pair is $(a_2,b_2)=(4,-7)$. The basis pair remains the $k=2$ harmonic; only the weights are being read off.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sine and Cosine Basis Functions",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting a Periodic Signal as a Weighted Sum of Harmonic Basis Functions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret the signal $x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+b_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$ as a weighted sum of harmonic basis functions.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the expanded trigonometric Fourier series. Start by separating the DC term from the harmonic terms, then group each cosine and sine pair by its shared harmonic index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+b_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $k=1$ cosine and sine terms form one weighted basis pair, and the $k=2$ cosine and sine terms form the next weighted basis pair. The coefficients stay attached to the correct function and harmonic index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\big[a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)\\big]+\\big[a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+b_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)\\big]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the periodic signal is the DC term plus a sum of weighted harmonic basis pairs. This is the reusable basis-function view: the sine and cosine terms are the basis functions, and the coefficients are their weights.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
